+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` has `resolvePointer` with a private `parse` for RFC 6901 pointers. RFC 6902 operations address
+locations by pointer (`path`, `from`), so parsing is shared. Inputs are untrusted JSON.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** RFC 6902 §4 semantics for all six operations; no mutation; atomic; safe with hostile keys; no dependencies.
+**Non-Goals:** diffing, merge patch, URI fragment pointers, in-place application.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Clone then mutate the clone.** `applyPatch` deep-clones the document once, applies operations to the clone and
+  returns it. Failure throws, so atomicity is free. Values taken from the patch (`add`/`replace` `value`) and from
+  `copy` are deep-cloned on insertion so no references are shared. A custom clone is used (not `structuredClone`)
+  so that `__proto__` members are copied as own properties via `Object.defineProperty`.
+- **Hostile keys.** Member writes use `Object.defineProperty` (never plain assignment) and reads use `Object.hasOwn`, so
+  `__proto__`, `constructor` etc. are ordinary names.
+- **Pointer parsing is reused** by sharing `parse` internally (not exported publicly); pointer errors are
+  wrapped as `JsonPatchError`.
+- **Validation up front.** The whole patch structure is validated (RFC 6902 §3) before any operation runs, so a
+  malformed patch fails regardless of earlier operations.
+- **Array indexes (§4.1, RFC 6901 §4).** Indexes must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*`; `-` is accepted only as the `add`
+  target (including the `path` of `move`/`copy`), and refers to the end. For `remove`/`replace`/`test`/`from`,
+  `-` and out-of-range indexes are errors.
+- **move** is `remove` at `from` then `add` at `path`; a `from` that is a proper prefix of `path` is an error (§4.4).
+  `from === path` is a no-op after verifying existence.
+- **test equality (§4.6).** Recursive deep equality: numbers by value, objects by same key set regardless of
+  order, arrays ordered, type must match.
+- **Ambiguity: remove of the root.** RFC 6902 does not say what results when the whole document is removed;
+  JSON has no "empty" value, so this throws rather than inventing `null`.
+- **Root operations.** `add`, `replace` and `test` with the empty pointer act on the whole document (§4.1, §4.3, §4.6).
+- **Extra members** in operation objects are ignored (§4: unrecognized members SHOULD be ignored).
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Deep cloning costs O(size) per call; accepted for the no-mutation guarantee.
+- Very deep documents could overflow the stack in recursive clone/equality; acceptable for typical payloads.
