+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Services need to change JSON documents with a standard, interoperable patch format. JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+is that standard, and it builds on the JSON Pointer support (RFC 6901) already in the library.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`: applies an array of operations
+  (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`) in order and returns the resulting document as a new value.
+- The input document and patch are never mutated; the result shares no structure with the input.
+- It throws when the patch is invalid (not an array, malformed operation, unknown `op`, invalid pointer) or when an
+  operation fails (target missing, `test` mismatch, etc.). Application is atomic: on error nothing is returned.
+- Add an exported `JsonPatchError` class so callers can tell these errors apart.
+- Hostile keys such as `__proto__` are treated as ordinary member names and never touch prototypes.
+
+Governing standards: RFC 6902, JSON Patch (§3 document structure, §4 operations, §5 error handling);
+RFC 6901, JSON Pointer (§3 syntax, §4 evaluation) for the `path` and `from` members.
+Out of scope: JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), patch generation/diffing, the URI fragment pointer form, and the
+`application/json-patch+json` media type handling.
+
+Nothing is **BREAKING**: this only adds new exports. `resolvePointer` and `JsonPointerError` are unchanged.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: applying an RFC 6902 JSON Patch to a JSON document.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+## Impact
+
+- Changed: `src/index.ts` (new exports, pointer parsing is reused internally); new tests under `test/`.
+- No dependency or `package.json` change.
+- New public API for importing projects: `applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`.
